1
00:00:01,450 --> 00:00:06,720
This is Reece Barker. British, raised in Munich, studying in the Netherlands.

2
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:10,600
He writes software, builds hardware, and co-founded a startup.

3
00:00:10,600 --> 00:00:13,640
But the easiest way to understand him is to start at the beginning.

4
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:18,130
Reece started primary school in West Sussex, on the south coast of England,

5
00:00:18,130 --> 00:00:23,090
before his family moved to Munich. That's where he grew up, at Munich International School,

6
00:00:23,090 --> 00:00:26,830
and where German went from a school subject to a second language.

7
00:00:26,830 --> 00:00:30,690
He speaks it at B2 level today, and he's still working on it.

8
00:00:30,690 --> 00:00:35,950
Outside class he volunteered in the school's Makers Lab, helping other students build their ideas,

9
00:00:35,950 --> 00:00:41,980
and designed laser-cut trophies for the cubing club. He wrote his first lines of code at twelve.

10
00:00:41,980 --> 00:00:47,330
At fifteen he shipped his first real product, a real-time extension for a web framework,

11
00:00:47,330 --> 00:00:51,230
and recorded a thirty minute walkthrough explaining exactly how it worked.

12
00:00:52,060 --> 00:00:58,060
For his IB Diploma he took Computer Science, German and Geography at higher level.

13
00:00:58,060 --> 00:01:04,820
And it was Geography that changed everything. His Geography teacher made the subject come alive.

14
00:01:04,820 --> 00:01:10,080
She showed him how the planet actually works, and how much is at stake.

15
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:15,280
Reece is the first to say that without her, he wouldn't be doing what he does today.

16
00:01:15,280 --> 00:01:21,690
His extended essay looked at London's Ultra Low Emission Zone, and whether it really cut CO2.

17
00:01:21,690 --> 00:01:26,710
It was the first time his two interests met. Data, and the environment.

18
00:01:27,450 --> 00:01:29,070
At sixteen he went freelance.

19
00:01:29,670 --> 00:01:35,640
Over almost three years he built full-stack web platforms with secure logins and live monitoring.

20
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:40,330
He also built a support and ticketing system for online communities in the entertainment space,

21
00:01:40,330 --> 00:01:45,880
connected to the APIs they already relied on, and sold it commercially on two marketplaces.

22
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:50,090
For the flow meter manufacturer Bronkhorst, he built a customer configurator

23
00:01:50,090 --> 00:01:54,740
that checks real physics constraints as you type. He delivered it in a week.

24
00:01:54,740 --> 00:02:00,190
Alongside all that, he spent three summers in the IT department at Munich International School.

25
00:02:00,190 --> 00:02:05,400
Today he's back there on contract, supporting IT and building on Microsoft's Power Platform,

26
00:02:05,400 --> 00:02:08,680
including the purchase order system the finance team works with.

27
00:02:08,680 --> 00:02:13,830
He also built his own browser extension for the IT team, to scan the school's iPads in and out,

28
00:02:13,830 --> 00:02:17,880
match them against class lists, and turn it all into a report automatically.

29
00:02:17,880 --> 00:02:22,060
In 2024 he moved to the Netherlands to study Applied Computer Science

30
00:02:22,060 --> 00:02:25,790
at Saxion University of Applied Sciences in Enschede.

31
00:02:25,790 --> 00:02:31,450
His first year ended with his propaedeutic diploma, cum laude. The degree goes deep.

32
00:02:31,450 --> 00:02:38,720
Embedded C and C++, real-time operating systems, signal processing. In his first year he helped build

33
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:43,450
an autonomous robot that explores a maze and finds the shortest way out on its own.

34
00:02:43,450 --> 00:02:46,810
And in one project, his two old interests met again.

35
00:02:46,810 --> 00:02:52,080
He built a Raspberry Pi that listens to passing traffic and profiles it by sound alone,

36
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:57,050
to understand emissions without a single camera. It scored nine out of ten.

37
00:02:57,050 --> 00:03:01,110
At Saxion's International Project Week, he earned the highest distinction.

38
00:03:01,110 --> 00:03:07,100
Now he's in the Industrial Automation minor, where a team of thirteen is designing a full production line,

39
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:13,880
with eight ABB robots, vision systems and PLCs, to assemble a LEGO bulldozer every five minutes.

40
00:03:13,880 --> 00:03:19,410
Alongside it, he's helping build a machine that sorts loose LEGO parts, by colour, by size,

41
00:03:19,410 --> 00:03:26,050
and with a camera that recognises each piece. Next comes a European Project Semester at TH Augsburg,

42
00:03:26,050 --> 00:03:29,480
then an internship and a graduation project in industry.

43
00:03:30,340 --> 00:03:33,650
He was also selected for the Saxion Honours Programme,

44
00:03:33,650 --> 00:03:37,600
a track for students who want more than the standard curriculum.

45
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:41,390
For Reece it's been about people as much as projects.

46
00:03:41,390 --> 00:03:46,910
Working with students from completely different fields. Building a real social circle.

47
00:03:46,910 --> 00:03:50,120
Learning skills far outside computer science,

48
00:03:50,120 --> 00:03:54,500
and asking bigger questions about who he is and what actually matters.

49
00:03:54,500 --> 00:04:01,920
He even chose an honest question to work on for the year. Why does he perform best when the pressure hits?

50
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:07,480
One moment stood out. His lecture on sustainable software, Smart Tech, Greener Planet,

51
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:11,130
was the moment all three of his peers picked out.

52
00:04:11,880 --> 00:04:16,400
Out of Saxion also came his startup, co-founded with three classmates.

53
00:04:16,400 --> 00:04:20,380
The idea starts with something simple. Every room has a climate.

54
00:04:20,380 --> 00:04:26,630
Temperature, humidity, air quality, light. Their AI connects to the systems a building already has,

55
00:04:26,630 --> 00:04:30,040
and controls them together, adjusting to who is actually there.

56
00:04:30,040 --> 00:04:33,380
At home, that's comfort with less wasted energy.

57
00:04:33,380 --> 00:04:37,920
In industry, it's stable conditions for people, products and machines.

58
00:04:37,920 --> 00:04:42,700
And in healthcare, where it matters most, it's a room tuned to each patient,

59
00:04:42,700 --> 00:04:45,040
to support better care and recovery.

60
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:49,000
The team has already laid the legal groundwork and a twelve month roadmap,

61
00:04:49,000 --> 00:04:53,970
and they're working toward their second prototype using funding from the Saxion Entrepreneurship Programme

62
00:04:53,970 --> 00:04:57,360
which awarded them €2,500 in initial funding.

63
00:04:57,360 --> 00:05:01,920
Reece belongs to the first generation to grow up alongside modern AI.

64
00:05:01,920 --> 00:05:07,620
He uses large language models every day, to build, to research and to learn faster.

65
00:05:07,620 --> 00:05:13,740
And he's studied what happens underneath, with electives in machine learning, deep learning and TinyML.

66
00:05:13,740 --> 00:05:19,390
Using it that much has taught him exactly where it falls short. It can be confidently wrong.

67
00:05:19,390 --> 00:05:24,990
It needs clear limits, and people who check the work. That's why he wants to help build it well.

68
00:05:24,990 --> 00:05:28,980
He believes AI can do real good for the planet and for society,

69
00:05:28,980 --> 00:05:31,780
from cutting wasted energy to improving care,

70
00:05:31,780 --> 00:05:37,110
as long as the people building it choose that path on purpose, and steer clear of the darker one.

71
00:05:37,930 --> 00:05:42,810
Ask the people around Reece what he's like, and you'll hear the same few things.

72
00:05:42,810 --> 00:05:49,190
He's easy to be around, he's funny, and he's someone you can count on. He's close with his family,

73
00:05:49,190 --> 00:05:54,940
and keeps a circle of friends he trusts completely. Underneath all of it is his faith.

74
00:05:55,580 --> 00:06:03,360
You see it in how he works and how he treats people. Being honest when it's inconvenient. Being patient.

75
00:06:03,360 --> 00:06:09,430
Doing good work even when nobody is checking. And helping people because it's the right thing to do.

76
00:06:10,250 --> 00:06:14,850
So what does he want next? It's simple to say, and harder to do.

77
00:06:14,850 --> 00:06:19,240
As a sustainability ambassador, he wants his work to match what he believes.

78
00:06:19,240 --> 00:06:24,530
Projects that genuinely make people's lives better, and leave the planet in better shape than he found it.

79
00:06:24,530 --> 00:06:30,590
Software, hardware, automation, AI. For him, they're all tools for the same goal.

80
00:06:30,940 --> 00:06:35,220
If you'd like to work with him, or just talk, you'll find him at reece.page.
